-- Q4436: Independence [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The former partner influences the financial statements and still receives firm-linked retirement benefits","The former partner no longer maintains any financial relationship with the firm","The former partner works in a department that is unrelated to the accounting function","The former partner departed from the firm more than one year before joining the client"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Independence is impaired when a former partner joins an audit client in a key position (such as controller) and retains financial ties to the firm, like retirement benefits dependent on firm revenues. Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct, the combination of influence over financial statements and ongoing financial interests creates familiarity and self-interest threats that cannot be mitigated.
Wrong (B): The absence of a financial relationship would actually support independence; the threat arises precisely because financial ties still exist.
Wrong (C): Working outside accounting does not address the issue — the controller role here directly influences financial statements.
Wrong (D): The one-year cooling-off period alone does not resolve impairment when ongoing financial interests remain.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4436;

-- Q4443: Independence [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A direct interest is owned by the individual; an indirect interest is held through an intermediary","A direct interest is always material to the auditor; an indirect interest is always immaterial","A direct interest involves equity securities only; an indirect interest involves debt securities only","There is no meaningful distinction between the two under professional auditing standards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct, a direct financial interest is one owned directly by the individual (e.g., owning shares of stock), while an indirect financial interest is one held through an intermediary such as a mutual fund or trust. Direct interests impair independence regardless of materiality; indirect interests impair independence only if material.
Wrong (B): Materiality is not the defining distinction — the ownership structure (direct vs. through an intermediary) is what differentiates them.
Wrong (C): Both direct and indirect interests can involve equity or debt; the instrument type is not the distinguishing factor.
Wrong (D): Auditing standards explicitly define and distinguish between direct and indirect financial interests.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4443;

-- Q4446: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A questioning mind with alertness to conditions indicating misstatement and critical assessment of evidence","An assumption that management is dishonest until evidence proves otherwise to the contrary","A refusal to accept any management representations during the course of the engagement","A requirement to corroborate every management assertion with independent external evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 200, professional skepticism is an attitude that includes a questioning mind, being alert to conditions that may indicate possible misstatement due to fraud or error, and a critical assessment of audit evidence. It is maintained throughout all phases of the audit.
Wrong (B): Skepticism does not presume dishonesty; the auditor neither assumes management is honest nor dishonest.
Wrong (C): Refusing all representations goes beyond skepticism — management representations are a required form of audit evidence under AU-C 580.
Wrong (D): While corroboration strengthens evidence, standards do not require external corroboration for every single assertion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4446;

-- Q4449: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Applying relevant training, knowledge, and experience to make informed audit decisions","Using a mathematical formula to calculate appropriate audit sample sizes for testing","Adopting the client''s own assessment of the appropriateness of accounting treatments","Consulting with legal counsel before making any decisions on audit-related matters"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 200, professional judgment is the application of relevant training, knowledge, and experience within the context of auditing standards, accounting standards, and ethical requirements to make informed decisions. It is essential throughout all phases of the audit, from planning through forming the opinion.
Wrong (B): Sample sizes involve judgment but are not determined by a fixed formula — professional judgment encompasses far broader decision-making.
Wrong (C): The auditor must independently evaluate accounting treatments; relying on the client''s assessment would abdicate professional responsibility.
Wrong (D): While consultation may be appropriate in some cases, professional judgment does not require legal counsel involvement for all decisions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4449;

-- Q4453: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the written representation as sufficient evidence without performing further procedures","Treat the representation as one piece of evidence and perform additional corroborating procedures","Reject the representation outright and presume that management is being deceptive about disclosures","Issue a disclaimer of opinion since management representations alone are never reliable audit evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 200 and AU-C 550, while management representations are a required component of audit evidence, professional skepticism requires the auditor to treat them as only one source. The auditor should perform additional procedures to identify possible undisclosed related party transactions, such as reviewing records, examining board minutes, and inquiring of other personnel.
Wrong (A): Accepting representations without corroboration fails to maintain professional skepticism as required by auditing standards.
Wrong (C): Skepticism does not require rejecting representations or presuming deception — it requires a questioning mind, not distrust.
Wrong (D): Management representations are valid evidence; a disclaimer is not warranted merely because representations were provided.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4453;

-- Q4456: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Audit efficiency in completing fieldwork within the established budget and timeline","Professional skepticism due to familiarity bias from a long-standing client relationship","Proper sampling methodology in selecting items for substantive testing procedures","Adequate documentation practices in recording audit procedures and conclusions reached"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): The partner''s dismissal of fraud indicators based on a long-standing relationship illustrates a failure of professional skepticism caused by familiarity bias. Per AU-C 200, auditors who have worked with a client for many years may develop unwarranted trust, reducing alertness to potential misstatements. All fraud concerns raised by team members should be investigated.
Wrong (A): The issue is not about efficiency — the partner is ignoring legitimate fraud indicators, not managing the timeline.
Wrong (C): Sampling methodology relates to selecting test items, which is unrelated to overriding a team member''s fraud concerns.
Wrong (D): Documentation practices concern recordkeeping, not the substantive failure to investigate potential fraud indicators.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4456;

-- Q4461: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Gives disproportionate weight to initial information such as prior-year balances when evaluating figures","Selects sample items from only one population rather than stratifying across multiple groups","Uses only one type of audit procedure for all financial statement assertions being tested","Relies exclusively on client-prepared schedules without obtaining independent corroborating evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Anchoring bias occurs when an auditor gives disproportionate weight to the first piece of information encountered (the "anchor"), such as prior-year balances. Per AU-C 200, this cognitive bias can lead to insufficient adjustment of expectations for the current period, reducing the effectiveness of analytical procedures and the ability to detect misstatements.
Wrong (B): Selecting from only one population describes a sampling design flaw, not a cognitive bias related to initial information weighting.
Wrong (C): Using one procedure type reflects a lack of variety in audit approach, which is a methodology issue rather than anchoring.
Wrong (D): Over-reliance on client schedules relates to evidence quality concerns, not to the disproportionate weighting of initial information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4461;

-- Q4462: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Decline to perform the procedure since specific guidance from auditing standards is unavailable","Consult with external legal counsel before proceeding with any testing of the arrangement","Apply professional judgment based on the principles underlying the standards and conceptual framework","Follow the client''s preferred accounting approach for recording the milestone payment transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 200, when specific guidance is not available, the auditor should apply professional judgment based on the principles underlying auditing standards. This includes considering the objectives of the relevant standards, evaluating the specific facts and circumstances, and determining an appropriate course of action consistent with the conceptual framework and ethical requirements.
Wrong (A): The absence of specific guidance does not justify declining to perform a procedure — the auditor must still exercise judgment.
Wrong (B): Legal counsel may be helpful in some cases, but it is not the required first step when auditing standards lack specific guidance.
Wrong (D): Following the client''s preferred approach abdicates the auditor''s independent responsibility to evaluate the arrangement objectively.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4462;

-- Q4465: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Overtest high-risk areas at the expense of properly evaluating lower-risk account balances","Rely too heavily on outputs from audit software without applying independent professional judgment","Underestimate the importance of planning analytical procedures in the risk assessment phase","Overemphasize readily recalled or recent experiences rather than considering the full range of risks"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The availability heuristic causes people to overweight information that is easily recalled or readily available, such as recent or vivid events. Per AU-C 200, in auditing this can cause auditors to overemphasize risks they have recently encountered while underestimating less common but equally significant risks, leading to biased risk assessments.
Wrong (A): Overtesting high-risk areas describes resource allocation decisions, not a cognitive bias about how risk judgments are formed.
Wrong (B): Over-reliance on software outputs relates to automation bias, which is a different cognitive phenomenon than availability.
Wrong (C): Underestimating analytical procedures reflects a planning deficiency, not the tendency to overweight vivid recent experiences.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4465;

-- Q4468: Terms of Engagement [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Must be reissued every year without exception regardless of changes in circumstances","May remain in effect for multiple years but should be reassessed when circumstances change","Is only required for the initial year of a recurring audit engagement with the client","Must be revised annually to include updated fee estimates and staffing plan details"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 210, for recurring audits the auditor should assess whether circumstances require the engagement terms to be revised and whether there is a need to remind the entity of existing terms. A new letter each year is not always required, but the auditor should reassess and update it when scope, management, or other significant factors change.
Wrong (A): Auditing standards do not mandate annual reissuance — reassessment is required, not automatic reissuance.
Wrong (C): The engagement letter is not a one-time document; it must be reconsidered whenever circumstances warrant.
Wrong (D): While fees may be discussed, annual revision solely for fee updates is not the standard''s requirement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4468;

-- Q4469: Terms of Engagement [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always agree to the stepdown request in order to accommodate the client''s stated preferences","Evaluate whether reasonable justification exists and decline if the change limits scope improperly","Automatically withdraw from the engagement without any further evaluation of the circumstances","Agree to the change but include a disclaimer paragraph in the resulting review engagement report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 210, the auditor should evaluate whether there is reasonable justification for changing from an audit to a review (e.g., changed circumstances removing the need for an audit). If the auditor believes the change is intended to limit scope, avoid adverse findings, or circumvent reporting requirements, the auditor should not agree and should consider withdrawal.
Wrong (A): Automatically agreeing ignores the auditor''s responsibility to assess whether the request is a scope limitation tactic.
Wrong (C): Immediate withdrawal is not required — the auditor must first evaluate whether reasonable justification for the change exists.
Wrong (D): Adding a disclaimer does not address the underlying issue of whether the stepdown request is appropriately justified.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4469;

-- Q4472: Terms of Engagement [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Obtain copies of the predecessor auditor''s complete set of working papers and documentation","Determine the fee structure and billing arrangements the predecessor used with the client","Obtain information bearing on engagement acceptance such as matters related to management integrity","Request that the predecessor formally recommend the successor auditor to the audit client"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 210 and AU-C 510, communication with the predecessor auditor helps the successor evaluate whether to accept the engagement. The successor should inquire about management integrity, disagreements with management, reasons for the auditor change, and any fraud or illegal acts. Client permission is required before this communication occurs.
Wrong (A): Obtaining workpapers occurs after engagement acceptance, not during the acceptance evaluation phase.
Wrong (B): The predecessor''s fee structure is not relevant to the successor''s engagement acceptance decision-making process.
Wrong (D): Requesting a recommendation is not a purpose of predecessor communication under auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4472;

-- Q4473: Terms of Engagement [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Communicate the inherent limitations of an audit to management and those charged with governance","Reduce the overall audit fee by limiting the scope of required testing procedures to perform","Allow the auditor to skip certain required procedures when time or budget constraints arise","Shift all liability for undetected material misstatements from the auditor to entity management"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 210, the engagement letter states the audit provides reasonable, not absolute, assurance to ensure management and those charged with governance understand the inherent limitations. These include the use of sampling, the exercise of judgment, and the possibility that not all misstatements — particularly those involving concealed fraud or collusion — will be detected.
Wrong (B): The reasonable assurance language relates to audit limitations, not to fee reduction or scope restriction.
Wrong (C): The language does not permit skipping required procedures — it explains inherent limitations of a properly conducted audit.
Wrong (D): Liability allocation is not the purpose; the language clarifies realistic expectations about audit capabilities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4473;

-- Q4474: Terms of Engagement [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Determining the appropriate sample sizes to be used during substantive audit testing procedures","Selecting the specific audit procedures the engagement team will perform during fieldwork","Providing the auditor with access to all information relevant to the financial statement preparation","Establishing the materiality levels that will guide the overall audit approach and testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 210, management is responsible for providing the auditor with access to all information that management is aware of and that is relevant to the financial statements, including records, documentation, and other matters. This responsibility must be acknowledged in the engagement letter.
Wrong (A): Determining sample sizes is an auditor responsibility based on professional judgment and risk assessment, not a management duty.
Wrong (B): Selecting audit procedures is the auditor''s responsibility, driven by the assessed risks of material misstatement.
Wrong (D): Establishing materiality levels is an auditor responsibility that requires professional judgment about financial statement users'' needs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4474;

-- Q4477: Terms of Engagement [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The objective and scope of the financial statement audit engagement","Management''s responsibilities for the financial statements and internal controls","A detailed list of all specific audit procedures the engagement team will perform","The expected form and content of the auditor''s report to be issued"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 210, the engagement letter does not typically list all specific audit procedures. Procedures are determined based on the auditor''s risk assessment and professional judgment during the engagement and may evolve as fieldwork progresses. The letter addresses the objective, scope, responsibilities, and expected report form.
Wrong (A): The objective and scope of the audit are standard elements included in every engagement letter.
Wrong (B): Management''s responsibilities for financial statements and internal controls are explicitly required content.
Wrong (D): The expected form of the auditor''s report is a standard engagement letter element under AU-C 210.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4477;

-- Q4480: Terms of Engagement [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the auditor, since the engagement letter is the firm''s internal planning document","The auditor and the client''s external legal counsel to ensure enforceability of terms","Representatives of both the auditor and the entity such as management or governance","The auditor, management, the audit committee chair, and the entity''s outside counsel"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 210, the engagement letter should be agreed to by appropriate representatives of both the auditor (typically the engagement partner) and the entity (typically management or those charged with governance, as appropriate). Both parties sign to acknowledge and agree to the engagement terms.
Wrong (A): The engagement letter is not an internal document — it is a mutual agreement between the auditor and the entity.
Wrong (B): External legal counsel involvement is not required by auditing standards for the engagement letter agreement.
Wrong (D): This overstates the requirement — auditing standards do not mandate signatures from the audit committee chair or outside counsel.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4480;

-- Q4484: Terms of Engagement [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor guarantees detection of all fraud regardless of concealment or management collusion","The auditor will investigate fraud only when the client specifically requests such procedures","Fraud detection is solely management''s responsibility and falls outside the audit''s stated scope","The audit provides reasonable assurance of detecting material misstatements from fraud or error"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 210 and AU-C 240, the engagement letter should clarify that the audit is designed to provide reasonable assurance about whether the financial statements are free from material misstatement, whether caused by fraud or error. This sets appropriate expectations without guaranteeing detection of all fraud.
Wrong (A): No audit can guarantee detection of all fraud — reasonable assurance acknowledges inherent limitations of the audit process.
Wrong (B): The auditor has a responsibility to consider fraud risk on every audit, not only when specifically requested by the client.
Wrong (C): While management has primary responsibility for fraud prevention, the auditor has explicit responsibilities for detecting material fraud.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4484;

-- Q4488: Understanding the Entity and Environment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The most efficient staffing plan and personnel assignments for conducting the audit engagement","Business risks that may result in material misstatement of the entity''s financial statements","The entity''s marketing effectiveness and competitive positioning within its target markets","The predecessor auditor''s planned approach and methodology for conducting the prior engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 315, understanding the entity''s objectives and strategies allows the auditor to identify business risks — risks arising from significant conditions, events, or circumstances that could adversely affect the entity''s ability to achieve its objectives. These business risks may result in material misstatement if the financial statements do not adequately reflect the entity''s business reality.
Wrong (A): Staffing decisions are operational planning matters, not the purpose of understanding entity strategy and objectives.
Wrong (C): Marketing effectiveness is a business metric, not a primary concern of the auditor''s risk assessment process.
Wrong (D): The predecessor''s approach is relevant during engagement acceptance, not when assessing the entity''s strategic risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4488;

-- Q4490: Understanding the Entity and Environment [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity''s current marketing strategies and promotional campaign plans for the coming year","The entity''s employee vacation and leave policies and associated human resources procedures","Significant decisions, commitments, and events that may affect the financial statements directly","The entity''s IT infrastructure design and information technology systems architecture details"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 315, reviewing board minutes helps the auditor understand significant decisions, commitments, and events that may affect the financial statements, such as authorization of major transactions, litigation developments, dividend declarations, related party transactions, and changes in accounting policies.
Wrong (A): Marketing strategies are operational matters not typically captured in board minutes as audit-relevant information.
Wrong (B): Vacation and leave policies are routine HR matters that do not bear on financial statement risk assessment.
Wrong (D): IT infrastructure details are typically obtained through other risk assessment procedures, not board minute reviews.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4490;

-- Q4492: Understanding the Entity and Environment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It has no impact because derivatives are standard financial instruments requiring no special attention","It requires the auditor to withdraw since derivative transactions are inherently too complex","The auditor should understand the new business line including derivative types, accounting, and risks","The auditor should rely entirely on management''s representations about the derivative transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 315, when an entity enters a new business line involving complex transactions like derivatives, the auditor must obtain a thorough understanding of the nature of the transactions, the applicable accounting treatment (including fair value measurement and hedge accounting), the risks involved, and the entity''s controls. This understanding is essential for identifying risks of material misstatement.
Wrong (A): Derivatives involve significant estimation uncertainty and complexity that require special audit consideration and expertise.
Wrong (B): Complexity does not justify withdrawal — the auditor should obtain the necessary understanding or engage specialists.
Wrong (D): Relying solely on management representations without independent understanding violates professional skepticism requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4492;

-- Q4494: Understanding the Entity and Environment [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The reporting phase when the auditor forms conclusions and drafts the final audit opinion","The substantive testing phase when the auditor gathers evidence on account balances","The planning and risk assessment phase when the auditor identifies misstatement risks","The engagement completion phase when the auditor evaluates overall audit sufficiency"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 315, the auditor obtains an understanding of the entity and its environment primarily during the planning and risk assessment phase. This understanding forms the basis for identifying and assessing risks of material misstatement, which drives the design of further audit procedures. The auditor continues updating this understanding throughout the engagement.
Wrong (A): The reporting phase uses the understanding already obtained — it is not the primary phase for obtaining entity knowledge.
Wrong (B): Substantive testing executes procedures based on risks already identified, not where entity understanding is initially built.
Wrong (D): Engagement completion involves evaluating results, not the primary gathering of entity and environment information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4494;

-- Q4498: Understanding the Entity and Environment [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Related party transactions may not be at arm''s length, creating misstatement and disclosure risks","Related party transactions are always fraudulent and must be reported to law enforcement authorities","The auditor must approve all related party transactions before they can be properly recorded","Related party transactions always require the auditor to issue a qualified audit opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 550, related party transactions may not be conducted at arm''s length and therefore may not reflect fair market values or normal business terms. They create risks of material misstatement because the terms may be unusual, the transactions may lack economic substance, or required disclosures may be omitted.
Wrong (B): Related party transactions are not inherently fraudulent — many are legitimate business activities that simply require proper disclosure.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to approve or disapprove an entity''s transactions; the role is to evaluate and report.
Wrong (D): Related party transactions do not automatically require qualification — proper disclosure and accounting may result in an unmodified opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4498;

-- Q4501: Understanding the Entity and Environment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity''s marketing strategy and outreach efforts to attract new students to the district","The entity''s customer satisfaction metrics and stakeholder feedback survey results overall","The entity''s stock price performance and market capitalization trends over recent periods","Budgetary compliance, fund accounting structures, and legal restrictions on resource usage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 315 and GAGAS, governmental entities have unique environmental factors including budgetary compliance requirements (which may have legal consequences), fund accounting structures, restrictions on use of resources such as grant restrictions, and specialized reporting requirements under governmental accounting standards.
Wrong (A): Marketing strategy is not a primary environmental consideration for a governmental entity''s financial statement audit.
Wrong (B): Customer satisfaction metrics are operational measures not directly relevant to the audit of governmental financial statements.
Wrong (C): A county school district does not have stock or market capitalization — these concepts apply only to publicly traded entities.$EXPL$
